--TEST--
`Core\Response::text` writes to the same output `echo` does, so the two interleave in call order rather than accumulating in two buffers -- `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink` and `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink` binds a request's `echo` to the response body, so there is one
// place a body can be. § 4's body members add the `Content-Type` to that, not
// a second buffer -- which is observable as ordering: what a body member wrote
// and what `echo` wrote come back in the order they were written.
echo "one ";
Core\Response::text("two ");
echo "three";
echo "\n";

// The same claim under `Core\Out::capture`, which is § 5's redirection of that
// one output: a capture in force takes both writers or neither, and taking
// only `echo` would be the second buffer this design does not have.
var $captured = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    echo "a";
    Core\Response::text("b");
    echo "c";
});
echo $captured, "\n";
--EXPECT--
one two three
abc
